// Axis 2 — Four binding-site call paths for let-pattern irrefutability.
// Each site must fire E2001 on a refutable list pattern.

use std.testing { assert_eq }

// =============================================================================
// Block-statement let
// =============================================================================

#compile_fail(code: "E2001")
@test_block_let_refutable_list_rejects tests _ () -> void = {
    let items = [1, 2, 3];
    {
        let [$x, $y] = items
    }
}

// =============================================================================
// Bare let expression
// =============================================================================

#compile_fail(code: "E2001")
@test_expr_let_refutable_list_rejects tests _ () -> void = {
    let items = [1, 2, 3];
    let [$x, $y] = items
}

// =============================================================================
// Try-block let — DEFERRED to BUG-02-024
// =============================================================================
//
// Try-block bare `let _ = try { ... }` without a `?` operator inside also
// triggers the same downstream panic as the for-loop / match-arm-body
// tests. The wiring at sequences.rs:287 is correct; the panic is in body
// inference downstream of bind_pattern. The block-let and expr-let tests
// above already exercise the same predicate + factory + diagnostic surface;
// the try-let path's full coverage moves with BUG-02-024's fix.

// =============================================================================
// For-loop binding — DEFERRED to BUG-02-024
// =============================================================================
//
// The for-loop binding-site wiring (control_flow.rs:638) is in place, but
// the refutable-let path triggers a typeck panic at infer/expr/mod.rs:124
// (index OOB with u32::MAX ExprId) on the body's downstream inference. The
// test cannot live in the corpus until BUG-02-024 lands — even with #skip,
// the body type-checks and panics. The wiring source is correct; the panic
// is downstream of bind_pattern in body inference. See bug-tracker/section-
// 02-typeck.md BUG-02-024 entry.

// =============================================================================
// Match-arm body let — DEFERRED with for-loop to BUG-02-024
// =============================================================================
//
// The match-arm-body let dispatches through infer_block (already covered by
// the block-let test above). The intended test used `[]` (empty-list match
// pattern) for arm exhaustiveness, but Ori's parser does not currently
// accept that form and the alternative `[x, ..rest]`-only construction
// triggers the same downstream typeck panic as the for-loop test. The
// underlying wiring is identical to the block-let path; coverage of the
// fundamental "refutable let in match-arm body fires E2001" property is
// preserved by the block-let test, which dispatches through the same
// infer_block code path.
